## <h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Stand Up! – Lesson # 5</h2>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's a School Day!</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Stand Up!</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: text: Identify objects on the pages of picture books, using simple words.
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <b>Learning Outcome:</b>
      <ul style="margin-top: 4px; margin-bottom: 0;">
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Mediation: Can identify objects in picture books by pointing and using single words (e.g., pointing to a picture of a chair and saying 'chair').</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can name some objects on the pages of picture books during choral reading activities (e.g., pointing to and naming objects like 'pen' or 'book' on the pages of a picture book during choral reading activities).</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can point to objects in response to a prompt by the teacher during choral reading (e.g., pointing to objects, such as a 'window' or 'door' on a picture book page in response to the prompt, 'Where is it?' during choral reading).</li>
      </ul>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Good Morning Song" with simple TPR actions like 'stand up', 'sit down', 'touch your nose'. Students sing and perform actions. This activates prior knowledge of classroom commands.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a large picture book about a school day, pointing to objects and naming them clearly (e.g., "This is a book," "It is a chair"). The teacher models imperative sentences (e.g., "Stand up, please," "Listen, please") and focuses on the pronunciation of /s/ in 'school' and /l/ in 'listen'. The teacher models using polite language.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the objects and commands.
          <ul style="margin-top: 4px; margin-bottom: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of a desk) "Is this a book or a desk?" (Students respond: "Desk!")</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Performing "stand up") "Am I sitting or standing?" (Students respond: "Standing!")</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's a School Day!".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful picture book titled "My School Day" featuring illustrations of a classroom, a teacher, students, desk, chair, table, book, window, door. The register is simple, narrative, and engaging for Kinder children.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying objects, understanding simple instructions, and answering basic WH-questions (e.g., 'What is it?').</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
          <ul style="margin-top: 4px; margin-bottom: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the picture book aloud, pointing to each object as it is named (e.g., "Look! A nice big desk. And a chair!").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students point to the objects in the book when the teacher names them during choral reading (e.g., "Point to the window," "Point to the door").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks simple WH-questions: "What is it?" (pointing to a book) or "Where is the teacher?" (Students point and use single words or short phrases).</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice mediating simple information and instructions. Focus on imperatives (Stand up, Listen), Present Simple (It is a…), WH-questions (What is it? Where is it?), and vocabulary: chair, desk, table, book, window, door, teacher, student, listen, stand up, touch, open, nice, calm, near, away. Pronunciation: /s/ in 'school', /l/ in 'listen', syllable stress in 'teacher' / 'student', initial sounds /b/ 'book', /l/ 'listen', /t/ 'touch'. Pragmatic: Following instructions, Describing actions, Responding questions. Sociolinguistic: Use of 'Please'.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
          <ul style="margin-top: 4px; margin-bottom: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. "Name and Point" Choral Practice (Controlled):</b> The teacher holds up the picture book. The teacher points to an object (e.g., a chair) and says, "What is it?" Students respond chorally, "It is a chair." The teacher then says, "Touch the chair, please." Students physically touch a chair near them. This practices identification and following instructions.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "Where Is It?" Game (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher hides a small object (e.g., a toy book or pen) 'near' or 'away' from a desk. The teacher asks, "Where is the book?" Students search and respond, "It is near the desk!" or "It is away from the table!" This reinforces prepositions and WH-questions.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "Command Your Friend" (Pair Interaction / Mediation):</b> Students work in pairs. One student acts as the "speaker" and gives a simple command to their partner, using polite language: "Please stand up!" or "Touch your desk, please!" The partner performs the action. Students then switch roles. This is a foundational mediation activity as students are transmitting instructions to a peer.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's a School Day!".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task: "Command Simon Says Game" Project</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Students will actively participate in and mediate a simplified "Simon Says" game using classroom commands. The goal is for students to not just follow, but also *give* simple instructions to their peers, acting as the "Simon" character.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
          <ul style="margin-top: 4px; margin-bottom: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher explains the "Simon Says" game again, clarifying that only commands starting with "Simon says" should be followed.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Initially, the teacher acts as "Simon" and gives commands: "Simon says, stand up!" "Simon says, touch your book!" "Simon says, open your door (pretend)!" "Simon says, listen!" (Focus on pronunciation of /s/ in 'Simon' and 'says').</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, the teacher chooses a student to be "Simon." This student stands in front and gives *one* simple command to the class, mediating the instruction to their peers (e.g., "Simon says, please sit down."). The teacher provides support and prompts if needed.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students take turns being "Simon," practicing giving polite, clear imperative commands to the class. This demonstrates their ability to understand, interpret, and re-transmit simple instructions to others.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The focus is on students' ability to clearly articulate the command, use "Simon says" and "Please," and for the other students to accurately follow.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on students' ability to give and follow simple commands, and their participation in the "Simon Says" game.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student takes at least one turn being "Simon" and giving a simple, polite command from the learned vocabulary (e.g., "Simon says, please stand up!" "Simon says, touch your chair!"). Other students demonstrate their ability to follow the command.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
          <ul style="margin-top: 4px; margin-bottom: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation of target words and phrases (e.g., 'stand up', 'book', 'Simon says').</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Employs at least one classroom command and object name.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of instruction:</b> Command is understandable for peers to follow.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration/Participation:</b> Actively participates in giving and following commands, demonstrating mediating the game.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students choose a drawing of a happy face or a sad face in response to two prompts:
          <ul style="margin-top: 4px; margin-bottom: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I understand what Simon says?" (Happy face / Sad face)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Could I tell my friends what to do in Simon Says?" (Happy face / Sad face)</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-8 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
          <ul style="margin-top: 4px; margin-bottom: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite command to say or do in Simon Says?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or a little hard to tell your friends what to do?" (Focus on the mediation aspect of giving commands).</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
          <ul style="margin-top: 4px; margin-bottom: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in the "Simon Says Game" for promoting mediation and communication? Did most students understand and successfully give commands to their peers?</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds might be needed for students who struggled to formulate or mediate simple commands? (e.g., providing command cards, more modeling).</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Stand Up!" theme by integrating all skills through the "Simon Says Game" project, serving as a comprehensive review and application of classroom commands and object identification. The next lesson will begin a new theme.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>